A||N||6||grinthock||Paint Removal||||1||
Z||000000||grinthock||08-02-2001||08:46 PM||||Hi guys I have a friend with a IROC-Z he just got, 1985, it has 3 LAYERS of paint on it (Yep 3 LAYERS!)   what needs to be done before it's painted we want to do all the prep ourselveS?  Do we remove all the paint layers?  If so what's the best method... Thanks in advance<p>[This message has been edited by grinthock (edited 08-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||08-02-2001||10:24 PM||||you could just paint over it again, but if you want a really good job, your gonna have to use stripper and wire brushes. very very messy. tape up seams with aluminum tape you can buy at auto body supply. the best way though is to find someone in your area that uses plastic pellets or walnut shells to blast it clean. also know in advance if you do either and the cars got prior body work, itll have to be redone.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Santa Cruzer||08-02-2001||11:08 PM||||did you try sandblasting it?<P>------------------<BR>BlOw bIg cHuNkS oF aMeRiCaN v6 YoU rIcE CrApEr'S! <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierose_1986/se.html" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/fierose_1986/se.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||maryjane||08-02-2001||11:45 PM||||Wouldn't even consider sandblasting, end up with more work than ever. Never get all the sand out of places ya didn't even know you had places.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tigger||08-03-2001||12:17 AM||||I'd probably use aircraft stripper.<P>I wouldn't use it on the plastic and urethane parts, just wet sand or DA those.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 08-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||08-03-2001||02:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>Wouldn't even consider sandblasting, end up with more work than ever. Never get all the sand out of places ya didn't even know you had places.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>thats why you dont use sand. plastic beads and walnut shells is what everyone i know has done for restorations. Ya ,if you strip it,use the metal tape aroung the edges of front and rear urathane bumpers. over lap it on body a 1/4 inch or so. the stripper gets 'into' the plastic and will ruin it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||08-03-2001||03:13 PM||||Like someone posted; you'll have to do bodywork over again if you use stripper. That part sucks because you may have areas that were bondo'd that look real good. Well, the stripper will remove that bondo of course.||||reg||1
